  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose an Acorns Alternative",
    "body": "Selecting an alternative to Acorns comes down to how your account balance interacts with flat monthly fees versus percentage advisory rates. Acorns charges a flat dollar fee ($4, $8, or $12 per month) regardless of whether your investment balance is $50 or $50,000. On small balances, flat fees create high percentage drag. For example, $4 per month equals $48 per year. On a $500 balance, that $48 fee represents 9.6% of your assets annually. On a $2,000 balance, $48 per year represents 2.4% annually. Conversely, on a $50,000 balance, $48 per year is less than 0.10%.\n\nPercentage-based alternatives charge an annual assets under management (AUM) fee. Wealthfront charges 0.25% per year, meaning an account balance of $2,000 costs $5 per year. Betterment charges $5 per month ($60 per year) on balances under $24,000, but drops to 0.25% per year if you establish recurring deposits of $200 per month or cross the $24,000 mark. At $24,000, Betterment's 0.25% annual fee comes to exactly $60 per year.\n\nZero-fee alternatives present a third cost model. Fidelity Go charges a $0 advisory fee on balances under $25,000, charging 0.35% per year only once your balance reaches $25,000. Schwab Intelligent Portfolios charges no advisory fee or commissions at all, but requires a $5,000 minimum opening deposit and maintains a cash allocation deposited at Charles Schwab Bank, an institution insured by the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC). Schwab notes that this cash deposit can pay a lower yield than cash alternatives outside the program.\n\nEvaluate these four factors before choosing a platform:\n\n- Account balance size: Compare the flat monthly fee against an AUM percentage to see which takes fewer dollars out of your portfolio each year.\n- Deposit routines: Determine if you plan to deposit spare change through automated round-ups, schedule fixed recurring deposits, or transfer lump sums.\n- Account types needed: Check whether you need an individual taxable brokerage account, an individual retirement account (IRA) such as a traditional, Roth, or SEP IRA, or a custodial account for children.\n- Cash and banking features: Identify whether you need an integrated checking account with debit card access, fee-free automated teller machines (ATMs), or a separate cash reserve with an annual percentage yield (APY)."
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "Stash Subscription Plans and Assets Under Management Fees",
      "body": "Stash offers an automated and self-directed investing platform through The Stash Plan, which costs $12 per month, or $108 per year when billed annually (a 25% savings). Stash includes 24/7 financial guidance, a personalized investment plan, Stock-Back rewards worth up to $120 per year, a $10,000 life insurance policy, and a 3% retirement match on contributions worth up to $225 per year. Stash offers a 7-day trial and states no account minimums on its pricing page. It supports taxable brokerage accounts, retirement accounts, and custodial accounts for children. Users can pick individual stocks and exchange-traded funds (ETFs) or use managed diversified portfolios.\n\nIn addition to the subscription, Stash charges an annual AUM fee of 0.25% on Smart Portfolios with balances of $1,000 or more, as well as on Managed Retirement Accounts. This means a customer using a managed portfolio pays both the subscription and the percentage fee.\n\nConsider an investor with a $5,000 balance in a managed portfolio. On Stash, annual subscription billing costs $108 per year, while the 0.25% AUM fee on $5,000 adds $12.50 per year, bringing the total annual cost to $120.50. On monthly billing ($12 per month), the annual total is $144 plus $12.50, which equals $156.50. On Acorns Bronze, that same $5,000 balance costs $48 per year ($4 per month), while Acorns Gold costs $144 per year ($12 per month) with no additional percentage advisory fee.\n\nStash falls short of Acorns Bronze on base pricing, since Stash costs $12 per month compared to $4 per month for Acorns Bronze. Stash also adds a 0.25% advisory fee on top of its subscription for managed accounts over $1,000, whereas Acorns includes portfolio management inside its subscription. Stash fits investors who want to combine self-directed stock picks with automated portfolios, use Stock-Back debit card rewards, and access a bundled life insurance policy."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Betterment Account Fees and Balance Thresholds",
      "body": "Betterment provides automated investing portfolios across individual taxable accounts, traditional IRAs, Roth IRAs, SEP IRAs, Solo 401(k) accounts, and self-directed investing. Its entry pricing lists a base fee of $5 per month for investing balances between $0 and $24,000. That flat fee switches to a 0.25% annual fee if the investor sets up monthly recurring deposits of $200 or more, or when the account balance reaches $24,000. Betterment also offers a Premium tier charging 0.65% annually (composed of a 0.25% Digital base fee plus a 0.40% Premium fee) for accounts with at least $100,000 in eligible household investments, which provides access to an advisor. Betterment Cash Reserve is a high-yield cash account with no fees, no stated balance minimums, and FDIC insurance through program banks. It offers a variable 3.50% APY as of September 21, 2026, alongside a 0.75% APY Boost for new clients who make a qualifying deposit through April 16, 2027.\n\nThe cost difference between Acorns and Betterment depends directly on account balance and deposit behavior. On a $2,000 balance with no recurring deposit, Betterment charges $5 per month ($60 per year), which represents a 3.0% annual drag. Acorns Bronze charges $4 per month ($48 per year), representing a 2.4% drag. However, if the investor sets up a $200 monthly deposit, Betterment's fee drops to 0.25% annually. On that $2,000 balance, 0.25% equals $5 per year, saving $43 per year compared to Acorns Bronze.\n\nOn larger accounts, Betterment's percentage structure changes the equation. An account with $30,000 pays 0.25% on Betterment, which equals $75 per year. On Acorns Bronze, that same $30,000 account costs $48 per year. On Acorns Silver, it costs $96 per year ($8 per month), and on Acorns Gold, it costs $144 per year ($12 per month).\n\nBetterment falls short of Acorns on micro-investing tooling, as Betterment does not offer debit card transaction round-ups or partner brand shopping bonus investments. Betterment fits investors who can commit to $200 monthly deposits to unlock a 0.25% fee, access tax-loss harvesting, and manage multiple retirement account types."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Wealthfront Flat Percentage Advisory Fee and Cash Account",
      "body": "Wealthfront offers automated portfolio management called The Wealthfront Portfolio for an annual advisory fee of 0.25% of assets under management. The fee covers automated daily rebalancing and tax-loss harvesting. Wealthfront also offers a self-directed Brokerage Account for individual stocks and ETFs, alongside a Cash account that requires $1 to get started and charges no account fees.\n\nBecause Wealthfront charges an advisory percentage rather than a subscription, small accounts pay substantially less in dollar terms. An investor with an account balance of $2,000 pays 0.25% per year, which totals $5 per year. On Acorns Bronze ($4 per month), the annual fee is $48. Moving to Wealthfront saves that investor $43 per year on portfolio management fees. On an account balance of $10,000, Wealthfront charges $25 per year, while Acorns Bronze still charges $48 per year. The break-even point between Acorns Bronze and Wealthfront arrives at $19,200. At $19,200, a 0.25% annual fee equals exactly $48 per year. Above $19,200, Acorns Bronze charges fewer total dollars per year than Wealthfront.\n\nAgainst Acorns Gold at $12 per month ($144 per year), the break-even balance on Wealthfront is $57,600 (0.25% of $57,600 equals $144 per year).\n\nWealthfront falls short of Acorns on checking integrations and micro-saving tools. Wealthfront does not provide spare-change round-ups from linked credit cards, nor does it offer shopping rebates or tip matching. Wealthfront fits investors who want automated index investing with tax-loss harvesting and no flat monthly fee."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Fidelity Go Zero-Fee Tier and Coaching Threshold",
      "body": "Fidelity Go provides robo-advisory portfolios with no minimum balance required to open an account. Investing begins once the account balance reaches $10. Fidelity Go charges a $0 annual advisory fee on account balances under $25,000. It publishes no trading fees. Once an account balance reaches $25,000 or more, Fidelity Go charges an annual advisory fee of 0.35% of assets under management. At the $25,000 mark and above, clients also receive unlimited one-on-one financial coaching sessions, along with tax-loss harvesting on taxable accounts holding stock-based Flex Funds.\n\nFor balances below $25,000, Fidelity Go costs less than Acorns across all tiers. An investor maintaining a $2,000 balance pays $0 per year on Fidelity Go, compared to $48 per year on Acorns Bronze, $96 per year on Acorns Silver, and $144 per year on Acorns Gold. An investor with a $15,000 balance pays $0 per year on Fidelity Go, saving $48 to $144 every year.\n\nOnce the account balance crosses $25,000, Fidelity Go's 0.35% advisory fee takes effect. On a $25,000 balance, 0.35% equals $87.50 per year. On a $40,000 balance, 0.35% equals $140 per year. By comparison, Acorns Bronze remains $48 per year and Acorns Silver remains $96 per year regardless of how large the balance grows.\n\nFidelity Go falls short of Acorns on lifestyle financial perks. Fidelity Go does not feature round-up deposit triggers, partner brand shopping bonuses, integrated family allowances, or will preparation services. Fidelity Go fits investors with account balances under $25,000 who want professional automated portfolio management with zero advisory fees."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Schwab Intelligent Portfolios Minimum Deposit and Cash Drag",
      "body": "Schwab Intelligent Portfolios builds and manages automated portfolios composed of approximately 50 expert-selected ETFs. The service charges a $0 advisory fee and $0 in commissions, though investors pay the underlying ETF operating expenses. It requires a minimum deposit of $5,000 to open an account. Automatic daily monitoring and rebalancing are included, and customer support is available 24/7. Accounts with $50,000 or more can enroll in automated tax-loss harvesting at no additional charge.\n\nA primary feature of Schwab Intelligent Portfolios is that a portion of every portfolio is held in cash deposited at Charles Schwab Bank, an FDIC-insured institution. Schwab states on its product page that this cash portion can pay a lower yield than cash alternatives outside the program. This lower cash yield functions as cash drag, which offsets the absence of a stated advisory fee.\n\nComparing expenses to Acorns highlights the starting barrier. Acorns allows investors to open accounts and start investing spare change with no large lump-sum deposit. Schwab requires $5,000 up front. For an investor who deposits $5,000, Schwab charges $0 in annual advisory fees. Acorns Bronze charges $48 per year on that same $5,000, and Acorns Gold charges $144 per year. Over a five-year holding period, avoiding Acorns Bronze's $4 per month fee saves $240 in advisory fees on that $5,000 balance.\n\nSchwab Intelligent Portfolios falls short of Acorns for casual savers who cannot meet the $5,000 opening requirement, and it lacks micro-investing tools like round-up deposits. Schwab Intelligent Portfolios fits investors who have at least $5,000 to invest and want automated ETF rebalancing without ongoing advisory fees."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who This Is Not For",
      "body": "Automated micro-investing alternatives do not serve every financial requirement:\n\n- Active stock traders who want zero commission options trading or direct margin access should not use automated robo-advisors. Wealthfront and Betterment focus on long-term automated ETF portfolios, not day-trading execution.\n- Comprehensive wealth planning requiring dedicated Certified Financial Planner professionals across estate, trust, and complex corporate tax needs falls outside these platforms, except where Betterment Premium ($100,000 minimum) provides general advisor access.\n- Complete business entity banking and commercial treasury operations sit outside these consumer accounts. For business treasury solutions, review our [business bank accounts roundup](/roundup/best-business-bank-accounts/).\n- High-net-worth investors seeking complex private equity or direct real estate holdings must look to private wealth managers rather than algorithmic retail accounts."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Would Change Our Answer on Acorns Alternatives",
      "body": "Our comparison of Acorns alternatives would shift if providers change their fee schedules, deposit minimums, or account thresholds:\n\n- If Acorns introduces a percentage-based fee tier for small balances instead of its $4, $8, and $12 monthly subscriptions, the fee drag on balances under $2,000 would diminish.\n- If Fidelity Go alters its $25,000 free advisory threshold or increases its 0.35% advisory rate, the fee comparison for mid-sized portfolios would change.\n- If Betterment removes the $5 monthly flat fee on balances under $24,000 for accounts that lack a $200 recurring monthly deposit, it would become significantly cheaper for small, irregular savers.\n- If Schwab Intelligent Portfolios lowers its $5,000 opening minimum or alters how its cash allocation is structured, it would become accessible to entry-level micro-investors.\n- If Stash eliminates its $12 monthly base plan in favor of a low flat AUM fee, it would compete more directly with Wealthfront and Betterment."
    }
  ],
  "stillBetterFit": {
    "heading": "When Acorns Is Still the Better Fit",
    "body": "Acorns remains the better fit for investors who rely on behavioral nudges to save money. The Acorns round-up mechanism automatically tracks linked card transactions and invests the spare change into diversified portfolios without requiring manual transfers. Its Bronze plan ($4 per month) bundles automated investing, Acorns Later retirement accounts, paycheck splitting, educational courses, and a 1% match on new IRA contributions during the first year.\n\nFor families and broader banking needs, Acorns Gold ($12 per month) bundles features that would otherwise require multiple services. Gold includes checking accounts without overdraft fees, access to 55,000+ fee-free ATMs in the AllPoint network, a 3% IRA match on new contributions during the first year (up to $225 when maxing out the contribution), and investment accounts for children with a 1% match. It also provides the Acorns Early app and debit card for kids, free federal, state, and local tax filing through third-party providers, a $10,000 life insurance policy for eligible customers, and a no-cost Will valued at $299.\n\nWorkers who receive gratuities can use Invest My Tips to generate a personalized QR code, invest 100% of each tip received, and earn a 1% match on qualifying tips. When an investor takes full advantage of the 3% IRA match ($225 value) and third-party tax filing, the $144 annual cost of Acorns Gold can be offset by bundled benefits. Banking services for Acorns Checking are provided by Lincoln Savings Bank or nbkc bank, members FDIC."
  },
  "verdict": "Acorns packages micro-investing, retirement accounts, and family banking into monthly subscriptions of $4, $8, and $12. For account balances under $20,000, percentage-based alternatives like Wealthfront (0.25% AUM) or free tiers like Fidelity Go ($0 on balances under $25,000) cost significantly less per year.\n\nTo select an Acorns alternative for automated investing, calculate your projected annual fee across your balance, check whether you need banking or retirement accounts, and open the account that fits your target deposit.",
